# TLS certificates per request -- the equivalents of curl's --cert, --key
# and --cacert. See docs/http-file-format.md's "TLS certificates" section.
#
# For illustration only: the hosts and the certs/ files below don't exist,
# so sending these fails ("could not read file ..."). Point them at your
# own PEM files and server to try it. A relative path resolves against the
# directory holding http-client.env.json (here, this one).

###
# @name MutualTLS
# A client certificate and its private key in separate PEM files, for a
# server that requires mutual TLS. The server's certificate is checked
# against the system's root CAs as usual.
# @clientCertificate certs/client.pem
# @clientCertificateKey certs/client.key
GET https://mtls.example.com/api/status HTTP/1.1

###
# @name PrivateCA
# A server whose certificate is signed by a private CA: trust that CA on
# top of the system's root CAs. No client certificate is presented.
# @caCertificate certs/ca.pem
GET https://internal.example.com/api/status HTTP/1.1

###
# @name MutualTLSPrivateCA
# Both at once, with the client certificate and key bundled in one PEM
# file (so no @clientCertificateKey), and the directory taken from a
# variable -- it could just as well come from the environment file.
@certDir = certs
# @clientCertificate {{certDir}}/client-bundle.pem
# @caCertificate {{certDir}}/ca.pem
GET https://internal.example.com/api/status HTTP/1.1
